L2 gate — outage absorption, poison isolation, watermarks
2026-08-10 · TimeLakeDB at :1963 · agent on the compose network so
stopping the database does not also destroy the agent's networking
================================================================================

OUTAGE — 900,000 lines tailed LIVE (generated at 10,000/s over 90 s),
TimeLakeDB stopped for 60 s mid-stream

  queue depth at recovery: 20 segments (~240 KB each), 21 spool events
  after recovery:          0 segments — fully drained
  gate: rows 900000 · distinct 900000 · min 0 · max 899999 · EXACT

  The database was unreachable for a full minute while the source file
  kept growing at 10,000 lines/s. Nothing was lost, nothing duplicated,
  and the agent did not hammer a server that was not there — it spooled,
  waited, and drained FIFO on recovery.

POISON — 20,000 lines containing an unparseable line and an invalid
UTF-8 sequence

  agent: read=20000 shipped=19999 quarantined=2 bisects=0
  rows:  19998  (= 20000 - 2 bad lines, exactly)

  Note bisects=0. Both bad lines were caught by LOCAL validation before
  anything was sent, so the server never returned a 400 and the batch
  never had to be split. That is DESIGN.md §4.3 working as intended:
  bisect is the safety net, not the mechanism. The one-line discrepancy
  between shipped=19999 and rows=19998 is the watermark row, which is
  written through the same shipper.

  (Without atomic-batch handling of any kind, those two lines would have
  cost all 20,000 — the batch writes nothing if any line fails to parse.)

WATERMARKS — published into TimeLakeDB as ordinary rows

  SELECT stream, COUNT(*), MAX(watermark_ns), MAX(lateness_ns), MAX(violations)
    -> app · 8 rows · lateness 100000000 (100 ms) · violations 0

  The observed estimate converged to the configured FLOOR, which is the
  correct answer for this corpus: the generator emits in timestamp order,
  so real lateness is zero and the floor is what stops a brittle
  watermark that any jitter would violate. Zero violations means the
  completeness claim held for every one of the 900,000 lines.

  Cold start behaved as designed — the first claims lag by the full
  ceiling and tighten as samples arrive, so a restart never over-claims
  completeness at the moment someone is checking after an incident.

A GAP THE DRILL EXPOSED, FIXED HERE
  Lines quarantined at the MAP stage (unparseable, un-coercible) only
  incremented a counter; they never reached the dead-letter file, which
  was written solely by bisect. So an operator would see "2 quarantined"
  and have no way to learn WHAT was dropped. Every drop now records the
  line and the reason — the difference between a metric and a diagnosis.

WORKSPACE
  cargo test --workspace: 38 passed, 0 failed
    + queue: 5 (FIFO, adoption of a previous run's segments, refuse-not-
      drop when full, resume hysteresis, no temp litter)
    + watermark: 6 (cold start at the ceiling, tightening, late tails
      widening it, one pathological line not pinning it, monotonicity
      with counted violations, restore clamped to range)

  One off-by-one caught by its own test: the resume threshold used
  `bytes < max/2`, so a queue drained to exactly half stayed paused.

NOT CLAIMED AT L2
  Multiline joins — the one L2 item still outstanding. It is parser
  work, independent of the durability story these gates test, and it is
  called out rather than quietly skipped.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MULTILINE — added 2026-08-10, completing L2

  160,000 source lines carrying 100,000 records (every 5th record
  followed by a 3-line stack trace):

    agent: read=100000 quarantined=0 multiline_truncated=0
    rows:  100000 records · 20000 containing "at handler"
                           · 20000 containing "caused by"

  Every record arrived once, and every stack trace arrived attached to
  the record it belonged to rather than as three orphan rows.

  A category error caught while drilling: the first attempt combined
  multiline with the JSON parser and quarantined 20,000 records. That is
  correct behaviour — a JSON log is already one record per line, so
  joining continuation lines onto it can only produce invalid JSON.
  Multiline belongs with PLAIN text, which is where stack traces live,
  and the config comment now says so.

  The checkpoint interaction is the part worth knowing: a record spans
  several source lines while the tailer's offset advances past each of
  them, so progress is NOT recorded while a record is half-assembled
  (`Joiner::has_pending`). Checkpointing there would resume after lines
  that were never shipped. It costs a little rework after a crash and
  never costs a record.

  Bounds are enforced and counted: a line cap, a byte cap, and a timeout
  for the last record in a quiet file — which has no successor to close
  it and would otherwise sit in memory until the process exited.

  +7 multiline tests (join, disabled passthrough, timeout emission, line
  cap, byte cap, orphaned continuation kept, pending-state tracking).
